-- lib/task-graph.cub — the task graph, as data of the language. Its meaning, its rationale and the
-- rules for using it are docs/task-graph-design.md (Avik's design, in his words): read that first. This file holds
-- only what a node is and the views over nodes; a particular work declares its nodes over it (this
-- repository's: task-graph/hyperactivity.cub).
--
import "core.cub"
import "document.cub"
-- t64 (2026-11-12): The work as one graph of nodes. Done: goals and tickets are one kind of node
-- (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub); every declaration of the code is a node of the same graph, its reason computed
-- from the machine's reading `#uses` (a declaration with a reason mentions it), from the machine
-- naming it (src/), or stated in the comment above it as `(part of NODE)` or `(prerequisite to
-- NODE)` (tools/task-graph.py why, unexplained). Measured 2026-11-12 over every file: 4310 of 8312
-- declarations have no reason; stating the rest where the code's text establishes a reason is what
-- remains (docs/task-graph-design.md §6, point 1). Library API nothing uses (lib/std.cub's pow, fact, happly, the
-- univalence lemmas; lib/prelude.cub's untyped helpers) states none: whether each stays is open.
--
-- A node is a task (docs/task-graph-design.md §3). Its name is an English sentence saying what it is (a
-- ticket's is its tN); the identifier it is declared under is how code refers to it. Its parts-of are the nodes it is a child of:
-- the reasons it exists, its value being exactly what it serves (§4; there is no scalar). A node's
-- children always cover it completely: a leaf is resolved when its work is done, any other node
-- exactly when all its children are, with no further step at the parent. Where a node's children are
-- not certainly complete, one child is its residual ("determine and do everything else this node
-- needs"), ordinary work that shrinks as pieces are carved out of it. A node part of nothing is a
-- root, and is declared one (roots); the root of this work is what Avik wants.
-- A node's text: a goal's statement is the comment `name (date):` above its definition in
-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub; a node opened for a specific piece of work is named tN and its text is the one
-- comment `tN (date):` at the place in the code it concerns. Every node in the state is open;
-- resolving one removes it (and any parent it leaves with no children, after confirming the parent is
-- achieved); the history keeps it.
-- The one relation besides parts: `after`, pairs (n , ms): n's work cannot start until every node of
-- ms is resolved (waiting on a node is waiting on every leaf under it). The order of work is computed
-- from parts and after alone (§5): every order is a topological sort of after; among work that can be
-- done in either order, X goes before Y when everything finishing Y first makes possible, finishing X
-- first also makes possible, and more (enabling is conjunctive: a node waiting on several becomes
-- possible only when all are resolved). tools/task-graph.py priority computes it.
-- Essential context: `essential-context`, pairs (n , ds): documents (lib/document.cub: files of the
-- repository as objects of the code, a reference to a missing one failing the check) that are
-- essential context for working on n and on everything below it. The relation is deliberately loose:
-- "essential context", not "must be read before" (docs/task-graph-design.md §7).
data Node = node (name : List Word) (part-of : List Node)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- views (2026-11-12, t62)
-- Views of the work are terms over it: `declarations Node` is every node, and the functions here
-- read it. tools/task-graph.py evaluates them through the machine (its `view` command).
text-eq : List Word → List Word → Bool
text-eq nil nil = true
text-eq nil (cons y ys) = false
text-eq (cons x xs) nil = false
text-eq (cons x xs) (cons y ys) = if weq x y then text-eq xs ys else false
name : Node → List Word
name (node n ps) = n
parts : Node → List Node
parts (node n ps) = ps
-- some node of the list has p's name
named-in : Node → List Node → Bool
named-in p nil = false
named-in p (cons q qs) = if text-eq (name q) (name p) then true else named-in p qs
-- the nodes of the list that are directly part of p
children : List Node → Node → List Node
children nil p = nil
children (cons c cs) p = if named-in p (parts c) then cons c (children cs p) else children cs p
-- the list without a node whose name came earlier
distinct-from : List Node → List Node → List Node
distinct-from seen nil = nil
distinct-from seen (cons c cs) = if named-in c seen then distinct-from seen cs else cons c (distinct-from (cons c seen) cs)
-- the nodes directly part of some node of the list
children-of : List Node → List Node → List Node
children-of all nil = nil
children-of all (cons c cs) = list-append (children all c) (children-of all cs)
-- the frontier and every node under it within k levels
under-from : Nat → List Node → List Node → List Node
under-from zero all fs = fs
under-from (suc k) all fs = list-append fs (under-from k all (distinct-from nil (children-of all fs)))
-- every node under p: as many levels as there are nodes
under : List Node → Node → List Node
under all p = distinct-from nil (under-from (list-length all) all (children all p))
names : List Node → List (List Word)
names ns = list-map name ns
